--TEST--
`Core\Db\RolledBack` carries the string it was raised with as both `reason` and `message`, is caught by a `RuntimeError` clause and not by a `LogicError` one, and a `RolledBack` clause does not catch its sibling `Core\Db\DbError` — `rule:core-classes/db-transactions`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\RolledBack
// The class on its own, with no server anywhere near it: what this pins is the
// property `crates/nvs-hir/src/errors.rs` declares for it and which `catch`
// clause it reaches, neither of which any driver has a say in.
// db-a-roll-back-survives-a-catch-of-throwable.nvst is where the member that
// raises it is asserted against a real transaction.
use Core\Db\DbError;
use Core\Db\RolledBack;

final class Abandoned {
    // Giving up is the world saying no rather than a bug in the program, so it
    // lands in the parent that means exactly that.
    public static function clause(): string {
        try {
            throw new RolledBack("the basket no longer adds up");
        } catch (LogicError $wrong) {
            return "a LogicError clause";
        } catch (RuntimeError $right) {
            return "a RuntimeError clause";
        }
    }

    // The other half of the pair the sibling case asserts from its own side:
    // the two `Core\Db` names sit beside each other, so a clause written for a
    // rollback the program chose must not take a refusal the server made.
    public static function sibling(): string {
        try {
            throw new DbError("the server said no");
        } catch (RolledBack $wrong) {
            return "a RolledBack clause";
        } catch (RuntimeError $right) {
            return "not a RolledBack clause";
        }
    }

    // § 18's own property, and the one string it is: the reason the caller gave
    // is readable under both names, so a log line and a branch never disagree
    // about why the work was abandoned.
    public static function reason(): string {
        try {
            throw new RolledBack("the stock would have gone negative");
        } catch (RolledBack $abandoned) {
            if ($abandoned->reason == $abandoned->message) {
                return $abandoned->reason;
            }
            return "two different strings";
        }
    }

    // A frame between the throw and the caller that re-raises the same value
    // hands on the same reason — the property travels with the value rather
    // than being read off the frame that built it.
    public static function rethrown(): string {
        try {
            try {
                throw new RolledBack("the import was not good enough");
            } catch (RolledBack $passingThrough) {
                throw $passingThrough;
            }
        } catch (RolledBack $outside) {
            return $outside->reason;
        }
    }

    // And the root admits it, which is what makes an outermost handler able to
    // report a rollback it was not written for.
    public static function root(): string {
        try {
            throw new RolledBack("nothing was written");
        } catch (Throwable $anything) {
            return $anything->message;
        }
    }
}

echo Abandoned::clause(), "\n";
echo Abandoned::sibling(), "\n";
echo Abandoned::reason(), "\n";
echo Abandoned::rethrown(), "\n";
echo Abandoned::root(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
a RuntimeError clause
not a RolledBack clause
the stock would have gone negative
the import was not good enough
nothing was written
